// Each actor's own garbage collector, a part of the actors package: every actor
// carries a region of its own, in its Header, made with it. 'ActorGc' is that
// region's reference: its values live in the memory of the one actor that made
// them and are found, moved and freed by a copying collector that runs only
// between that actor's messages, when the actor's state and its parked 'await'
// records are the only roots. An actor allocates in its region by naming itself,
// with nothing imported beyond the actors package an actor already needs:
//
//   mod scene;
//   import actors;           // folds in 'ActorGc'; 'actors.GcStats' and the knobs are qualified
//
//   struct Node { pub n i64; pub next Option[ActorGc[mut, Node]]; }
//   alias NodeRef = ActorGc[mut, Node];
//
//   actor Keeper {
//     head Option[NodeRef];
//
//     fn init(self &new) { *self = new Self(None[NodeRef]); }
//
//     pub async do push(self, n i64) {
//       head = Some[NodeRef](new self[Node](n, head));   // in the actor's region
//     }
//   }
//
// 'new self[T](args)' is 'self.allocRegion().alloc(new T(args))': the compiler
// gives the state of every actor a method 'allocRegion', answering the actor's
// 'ActorRegion' (regionOf, below), and a value that has one leads to a region
// ('new x[T](...)', compiler/c/doc/nodes/literals.md, "Construction"). Only the
// actor's own methods, the behaviours among them, hold 'self', so only they
// allocate in its region; 'init' does after '*self' is filled. A reference into
// the region is made, held, copied and stored inside the actor, in its state, in
// locals, in a 'List' or an 'Rc' it owns, in a record parked by an 'await'; it
// is never Sendable, so it never leaves the actor in a message or a reply. A
// read-only one, from 'allocImm' (PROVISIONAL), is Shareable in a behaviour's
// 'parallel each', where nothing is allocated in the region.
// A collection made in the region, 'new self[List[T]](16)', takes the region's
// own allocate, resize and release for its block, as any CarriesRegion type
// made in a region does (A COLLECTION'S BLOCK, below). 'self.allocRegion().stats()'
// says what the region holds.
//
// WHAT AN ACTOR PAYS. The region is a field of the actor's Header, written when
// the actor is made, and holds no memory until the actor first allocates: the
// first chunk is made then. An actor that never allocates carries the record,
// and its message loop one flag test.
//
// HOW IT COLLECTS. The region is a list of CHUNKS the actor allocates in, a
// bump pointer through the newest. The first is 4 KiB, made when the actor
// first allocates; each next is twice the last, up to 1 MiB, or as large as a
// value that does not fit needs. A value over 4 KiB is a LARGE object, a
// block of its own that never moves. Every object has a 24-byte header:
// its type's record, its size, a link and some flags.
//
// A worker calls the collector after each message the actor handles (and
// after the rest of a behaviour that began a 'parallel each', once the loop
// has finished). It collects when the bytes allocated since the last collection
// exceed the bytes that survived it, and not under 64 KiB (the FLOOR), so a
// tiny actor does not collect constantly; 'setFloor' changes the floor for
// the process, 'setStress' collects after every message. The collection is
// Cheney's: the actor's state and each parked record are handed to the
// region's 'mark' reference by reference; 'mark' copies the object it finds
// into new chunks and rewrites the slot the reference was stored in; the new
// chunks are scanned in order, each object's references marked in turn, until
// nothing is left to scan. The old chunks are then freed whole. A large
// object is marked in place and scanned from a list; the unmarked ones are
// freed. There is no write barrier: nothing runs while it does.
//
// FINALIZERS. An object whose type needs finalizing (it has a 'final', or a
// field that does) is on the region's FINAL LIST from the moment it is made.
// When a collection finds one unreached, it is kept for one more cycle: it
// and what it reaches are copied like the rest, and its death runs on the copy,
// in the order the objects were made, newest first, once. So a 'final' reads
// the references its object holds, whose targets are there, finalized
// already or not; they are freed by the collection after, as the collector
// package's are. When the actor dies, after its state's own death, every
// object still on the final list is finalized the same way and the region is
// freed whole.
//
// A reference behind an 'Rc' is traced by the 'Rc' ('visit', once for each
// collection: the epoch is 1, 2, 3 ... never 0), so a cycle of them ends.
//
// A COLLECTION'S BLOCK. The region is also a MemRegion: its first field, 'region',
// points at the three functions (allocate, resize, release) that sit in the
// actor's Header beside the rest of the region, which never moves. A type
// declaring CarriesRegion, made in the region ('new self[List[i64]](16)'), is
// handed that region and takes its block from it, so the block is the actor's
// region's: counted in the bytes allocated since the last collection and in
// the bytes that survive one, and given back by the collection's own 'final'
// (the list's, which every list has). A block is NOT bump-allocated in the
// chunks and NOT copied by a collection: it is a block of its own, as a large
// object is, and the collection's 'ptr' is the only holder of its address, a
// raw pointer a collector has no way to rewrite (the compiler gives the
// collector no way to find a block field, so nothing says which word of a
// collection holds one). What a collection moves is the collection's own words
// (a List's four), a region object like any, which keep the block's address and
// the region's address, neither of which a collection changes. Resize is
// 'realloc', so an outgrown block goes back at once, not at the next collection.
// Because every collection has a 'final', one nobody reaches is finalized by the
// collector as any dead object is, and that gives its block back; at the actor's
// death, the same for every one still alive.
//
// WHAT TO KNOW. An object moves in every collection that finds it, so nothing
// holds its address across a message: a raw pointer to one is the program's own
// risk, as in any moving collector. A collection that runs between two messages
// sees no borrow. The region's own memory is the actor's: a collection is
// only ever run by the worker running that actor.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The knobs: set before the first actor allocates, for the process
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Bytes allocated since the last collection that a collection waits for, at the least
const defaultFloor = 65536usize;
// The first chunk of a region, and the most a chunk grows to unless a value needs more
const firstChunkBytes = 4096usize;
const maxChunkBytes = 1048576usize;
// A value over this many bytes (header included) is a large object, never moved
const largeBytes = 4096usize;

mut floorBytes = 65536usize;
mut stressOn = false;
mut envRead = false;

// Collect no sooner than after this many bytes are allocated: the floor under
// the rule "allocated since the last collection exceeds what survived it".
// For the process, before the first actor allocates
pub fn setFloor(bytes usize) {
  envRead = true;
  floorBytes = bytes;
}

// Collect after every message, whatever was allocated: for tests, which find a
// reference a collection missed, a stale one a move left behind, at once. The
// environment variable CONE_ACTORGC_STRESS does the same for a whole run
pub fn setStress(on bool) {
  envRead = true;
  stressOn = on;
}

fn readEnv() {
  if !envRead {
    envRead = true;
    imm val = libc.getenv("CONE_ACTORGC_STRESS");
    if val as usize != 0usize {
      stressOn = true;
    }
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The region: a header before each value
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

const flagLarge = 1u32;      // a block of its own, never moved: 'link' chains the large objects
const flagForwarded = 2u32;  // copied, in this collection: 'link' is where
const flagMarked = 4u32;     // a large object reached in this collection

// The header before every value. It is a collector's data, so its fields are
// public, but a program never holds one.
pub struct ActorGc is RegionRef, Traced, ActorLocal {
  pub ty *TypeRecord;  // the value's type record: size, alignment, finalizer, trace
  pub link usize;      // a large object: the next one; a forwarded one: its copy
  pub size u32;        // the object's bytes, header included, a multiple of 16
  pub flags u32;

  // 'new ActorGc[mut, T](v)', made through the actor's region ('alloc' of
  // ActorRegion), which names the actor's region first
  fn alloc(size usize, ty *TypeRecord) *u8 {
    imm sp = target;
    target = null;
    if sp == null {
      panic("ActorGc: a value is made in an actor's region by 'new self[T](...)', never by 'new ActorGc[...]' on its own");
    }
    (sp as &mut ActorRegion).allocate(size, ty);
  }

  // A reference was found, by the state, a parked record or an object being
  // scanned: 'slot' is where it is stored. The object is copied into the new
  // chunks, once, and the slot rewritten to point at the copy; a large object
  // stays where it is and is marked
  fn mark(self &uni ActorGc, slot *usize) {
    imm sp = curActorRegion;
    if sp == null {
      return;
    }
    (sp as &mut ActorRegion).markObject(self as *ActorGc, slot);
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The region's memory
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The space a collection is in, for 'mark'; and the space the next allocation is
// for. Thread-local: workers run actors, and so collect and allocate, at once
mut @threadlocal curActorRegion *ActorRegion = null;
mut @threadlocal target *ActorRegion = null;

// A chunk of the region: this header, then objects, each a multiple of 16
// bytes. 'next' is the next newer chunk.
struct Chunk {
  pub next *Chunk;
  pub bytes usize;     // the chunk's size, this header included
  pub used usize;      // the offset of its first unused byte
  pub pad usize;
}

// What a test or a tuning run reads of an actor's region
pub struct GcStats {
  pub collections usize;    // collections run
  pub objects usize;        // objects the region holds now, garbage not yet collected too
  pub bytes usize;          // their bytes, headers included
  pub chunks usize;         // chunks the region holds
  pub large usize;          // of the objects, the large ones
  pub copied usize;         // objects copied, in all the collections
  pub finalized usize;      // finals run, in all the collections and at death
  pub freed usize;          // large objects freed, in all
  pub allocated usize;      // bytes allocated since the last collection
  pub survived usize;       // bytes that survived the last collection
  pub blocks usize;         // bytes held now in the blocks of collections made in the region
}

// An actor's region: its chunks, its large objects, its final list, and what
// the collector counts. It is a field of the actor's Header, made with the actor
// and freed at its death, so it never moves. It is a region value (its first
// field is a public '*MemRegion', the region the collections made in it draw
// their blocks from: its own 'vt', the allocate, resize and release below, which
// sit beside it), and 'new self[T](...)', which is 'self.allocRegion().alloc(new
// T(...))', is the allocation below.
pub struct ActorRegion {
  pub region *MemRegion;         // what collections made here get their blocks from: 'vt', once the Header is in place
  vt MemRegion;                  // the region's three functions: SECOND, at the offset of one pointer (blockRegion)
  used bool = false;             // asked for, by 'allocRegion': the message loop looks at nothing else until then
  first *Chunk = null;           // the oldest chunk
  last *Chunk = null;            // the newest: allocation bumps here
  scanChunk *Chunk = null;       // a collection's scan: the chunk it is in
  scanAt usize = 0usize;         // and the offset in it
  large usize = 0usize;          // the newest large object's header, or 0
  finals *usize = null;          // the headers of the objects with a final, oldest first
  nfinals usize = 0usize;
  capfinals usize = 0usize;
  gray *usize = null;            // large objects reached in this collection, not yet scanned
  ngray usize = 0usize;
  capgray usize = 0usize;
  allocated usize = 0usize;      // bytes allocated since the last collection
  live usize = 0usize;           // bytes that survived the last one
  liveNow usize = 0usize;        // bytes a collection in progress has kept
  nobjects usize = 0usize;
  nlarge usize = 0usize;
  nchunks usize = 0usize;
  bytes usize = 0usize;
  ncollections usize = 0usize;
  ncopied usize = 0usize;
  nfinalized usize = 0usize;
  nfreed usize = 0usize;
  blocks usize = 0usize;         // bytes held in the blocks of collections made here
  epoch u32 = 0u32;

  // A region holding nothing: its three functions set, its 'region' still to be
  // pointed at them once the Header it is a field of is where it will stay
  fn empty() ActorRegion {
    new ActorRegion(null, new MemRegion(&blockAlloc, &blockResize, &blockRelease));
  }

  // A value made in the actor's region: 'new self[T](v)'. The next ActorGc
  // value made on this thread is in this region
  pub fn alloc[T](self &mut, v T) ActorGc[mut, T] {
    target = self as *ActorRegion;
    new ActorGc[mut, T](v);
  }

  // A value made in the actor's region that nothing changes: the reference is
  // read-only ('imm'), so a 'parallel each' written in a behaviour may read it
  // in its passes (it is Shareable, as a 'mut' one is not). 'new self[T]' lends
  // 'mut', all a region value lends; this is the way to a read-only one.
  // PROVISIONAL: the spelling (the design chat is to say whether a permission
  // goes in the brackets, 'new self[imm, T]', or this stays)
  pub fn allocImm[T](self &mut, v T) ActorGc[imm, T] {
    target = self as *ActorRegion;
    new ActorGc[imm, T](v);
  }

  // What the region holds and has done, for tests and tuning
  pub fn stats(self &) GcStats {
    new GcStats(self.ncollections, self.nobjects, self.bytes, self.nchunks, self.nlarge, self.ncopied,
      self.nfinalized, self.nfreed, self.allocated, self.live, self.blocks);
  }

  // ---- A collection's block: the region's allocate, resize and release ----

  // A block of 'bytes' bytes for a collection made in the region. Not in the
  // chunks, and never moved (A COLLECTION'S BLOCK, above): malloc's, counted
  // against the bytes allocated since the last collection
  fn blockNew(self &mut, bytes usize, align usize) *u8 {
    if align > 16usize {
      panic("ActorGc: a collection's block aligned past 16 bytes is not supported");
    }
    mut n = bytes;
    if n == 0usize {
      n = 1usize;
    }
    imm p = libc.malloc(n);
    if p == null {
      return null;
    }
    self.blocks = self.blocks + bytes;
    self.allocated = self.allocated + bytes;
    p;
  }

  // The block, 'old' bytes long, made 'new': realloc's, in place or not. A
  // growth is counted as allocated, as much as it grew
  fn blockGrow(self &mut, block *u8, oldBytes usize, newBytes usize, align usize) *u8 {
    if block == null {
      return self.blockNew(newBytes, align);
    }
    mut n = newBytes;
    if n == 0usize {
      n = 1usize;
    }
    imm p = libc.realloc(block, n);
    if p == null {
      return null;
    }
    if newBytes > oldBytes {
      self.blocks = self.blocks + (newBytes - oldBytes);
      self.allocated = self.allocated + (newBytes - oldBytes);
    } else {
      self.blocks = self.blocks - (oldBytes - newBytes);
    }
    p;
  }

  // The block given back: by the collection's own 'final'
  fn blockGive(self &mut, block *u8, bytes usize) {
    if block == null {
      return;
    }
    libc.free(block);
    if self.blocks >= bytes {
      self.blocks = self.blocks - bytes;
    } else {
      self.blocks = 0usize;
    }
  }

  // n rounded up to a multiple of a, a power of two
  fn roundUp(n usize, a usize) usize {
    (n + a - 1usize) & (0usize - a);
  }

  // Where the value of an object with this type record sits after its header:
  // the {region, permission, value} layout the compiler lays out
  fn valueOffset(rec &TypeRecord) usize {
    ActorRegion.roundUp(mem.sizeof[ActorGc](), rec.align);
  }

  // Room for an object of 'bytes' bytes (a multiple of 16): the newest chunk's
  // next bytes, or a chunk made for it
  fn bump(self &mut, bytes usize) *u8 {
    mut c = self.last;
    if c == null or (c as &Chunk).bytes - (c as &Chunk).used < bytes {
      c = self.newChunk(bytes);
      if c == null {
        return null;
      }
    }
    imm chunk = c as &mut Chunk;
    imm at = (c as *u8) + chunk.used;
    chunk.used = chunk.used + bytes;
    at;
  }

  // A new newest chunk with room for 'bytes': the first is small, each next
  // twice the last, to a most, unless the object needs more
  fn newChunk(self &mut, bytes usize) *Chunk {
    mut size = firstChunkBytes;
    if self.last != null {
      size = (self.last as &Chunk).bytes * 2usize;
      if size > maxChunkBytes {
        size = maxChunkBytes;
      }
    }
    imm need = bytes + mem.sizeof[Chunk]();
    if size < need {
      size = ActorRegion.roundUp(need, 4096usize);
    }
    imm c = libc.malloc(size) as *Chunk;
    if c == null {
      return null;
    }
    mem.writeRaw(c, new Chunk(null, size, mem.sizeof[Chunk](), 0usize));
    if self.last == null {
      self.first = c;
    } else {
      (self.last as &mut Chunk).next = c;
    }
    self.last = c;
    self.nchunks = self.nchunks + 1usize;
    self.bytes = self.bytes + size;
    c;
  }

  // A new object: 'size' is the compiler's, the header and the value. Null if
  // memory is out, or the value wants an alignment past 16
  fn allocate(self &mut, size usize, ty *TypeRecord) *u8 {
    imm rec = ty as &TypeRecord;
    if rec.align > 16usize {
      return null;
    }
    imm bytes = ActorRegion.roundUp(size, 16usize);
    if bytes > 4294967295usize {
      return null;
    }
    mut p *u8 = null;
    mut flags = 0u32;
    mut link = 0usize;
    if bytes > largeBytes {
      p = libc.malloc(bytes);
      flags = flagLarge;
      link = self.large;
    } else {
      p = self.bump(bytes);
    }
    if p == null {
      return null;
    }
    mem.writeRaw(p as *ActorGc, new ActorGc(ty, link, u32.from(bytes), flags));
    if flags == flagLarge {
      self.large = p as usize;
      self.nlarge = self.nlarge + 1usize;
    }
    if rec.flags & 1u32 != 0u32 {
      self.pushFinal(p as usize);
    }
    self.allocated = self.allocated + bytes;
    self.nobjects = self.nobjects + 1usize;
    p;
  }

  // The object with a final at 'header', put last on the final list
  fn pushFinal(self &mut, header usize) {
    if self.nfinals == self.capfinals {
      mut cap = 16usize;
      if self.capfinals != 0usize {
        cap = self.capfinals * 2usize;
      }
      imm grown = libc.realloc(self.finals as *u8, cap * mem.sizeof[usize]()) as *usize;
      if grown == null {
        panic("ActorGc: out of memory for the final list");
      }
      self.finals = grown;
      self.capfinals = cap;
    }
    *(self.finals + self.nfinals) = header;
    self.nfinals = self.nfinals + 1usize;
  }

  // A large object reached, not yet scanned
  fn pushGray(self &mut, header usize) {
    if self.ngray == self.capgray {
      mut cap = 16usize;
      if self.capgray != 0usize {
        cap = self.capgray * 2usize;
      }
      imm grown = libc.realloc(self.gray as *u8, cap * mem.sizeof[usize]()) as *usize;
      if grown == null {
        panic("ActorGc: out of memory for a collection");
      }
      self.gray = grown;
      self.capgray = cap;
    }
    *(self.gray + self.ngray) = header;
    self.ngray = self.ngray + 1usize;
  }

  // ---- The collection ----

  // A reference to the object at 'header' was found at 'slot'
  fn markObject(self &mut, header *ActorGc, slot *usize) {
    imm h = header as &mut ActorGc;
    if h.flags & flagLarge != 0u32 {
      if h.flags & flagMarked == 0u32 {
        h.flags = h.flags | flagMarked;
        self.liveNow = self.liveNow + usize.from(h.size);
        self.pushGray(header as usize);
      }
      return;
    }
    if h.flags & flagForwarded == 0u32 {
      self.copyObject(h);
    }
    *slot = h.link + ActorRegion.valueOffset(h.ty as &TypeRecord);
  }

  // Copy the object into the new chunks, and leave its forwarding address in
  // the old header
  fn copyObject(self &mut, h &mut ActorGc) {
    imm bytes = usize.from(h.size);
    imm dst = self.bump(bytes);
    if dst == null {
      panic("ActorGc: out of memory for a collection");
    }
    libc.memcpy(dst, h as *ActorGc as *u8, bytes);
    h.flags = h.flags | flagForwarded;
    h.link = dst as usize;
    self.liveNow = self.liveNow + bytes;
    self.ncopied = self.ncopied + 1usize;
  }

  // Hand the references the object at 'header' holds to the region's 'mark'
  fn scanObject(self &mut, header usize) {
    imm h = header as &ActorGc;
    imm rec = h.ty as &TypeRecord;
    if rec.flags & 2u32 != 0u32 {
      imm trace = rec.trace;
      trace((header + ActorRegion.valueOffset(rec)) as *u8, self.epoch);
    }
  }

  // Scan the new chunks in order, and the large objects reached, until nothing
  // is left to scan (Cheney's scan: copying appends to the chunks being scanned)
  fn drain(self &mut) {
    mut going = true;
    while going {
      going = false;
      if self.scanChunk == null and self.first != null {
        self.scanChunk = self.first;
        self.scanAt = mem.sizeof[Chunk]();
      }
      if self.scanChunk != null {
        imm c = self.scanChunk as &Chunk;
        if self.scanAt < c.used {
          imm at = (self.scanChunk as usize) + self.scanAt;
          self.scanAt = self.scanAt + usize.from((at as &ActorGc).size);
          self.scanObject(at);
          going = true;
        } elif c.next != null {
          self.scanChunk = c.next;
          self.scanAt = mem.sizeof[Chunk]();
          going = true;
        }
      }
      if !going and self.ngray > 0usize {
        self.ngray = self.ngray - 1usize;
        self.scanObject(*(self.gray + self.ngray));
        going = true;
      }
    }
  }

  // Has a collection found the object at 'header'?
  fn reached(header usize) bool {
    ((header as &ActorGc).flags & (flagForwarded | flagMarked)) != 0u32;
  }

  // The object's header now: where it was copied, or where it stayed
  fn moved(header usize) usize {
    if (header as &ActorGc).flags & flagLarge != 0u32 {
      return header;
    }
    (header as &ActorGc).link;
  }

  // The objects with a final that nothing reached: each is kept one more
  // cycle, copied with all it reaches, and its death run on the copy, newest
  // first, once. Those that were reached stay on the list, at their new
  // addresses.
  fn finalizeDead(self &mut) {
    if self.nfinals == 0usize {
      return;
    }
    // Which are dead is judged before any is kept: keeping one reaches the
    // others. The list keeps those reached, in order, at their new addresses
    imm dead = libc.malloc(self.nfinals * mem.sizeof[usize]()) as *usize;
    if dead == null {
      panic("ActorGc: out of memory for a collection");
    }
    mut ndead = 0usize;
    mut kept = 0usize;
    mut i = 0usize;
    while i < self.nfinals {
      imm header = *(self.finals + i);
      if ActorRegion.reached(header) {
        *(self.finals + kept) = ActorRegion.moved(header);
        kept = kept + 1usize;
      } else {
        *(dead + ndead) = header;
        ndead = ndead + 1usize;
      }
      i = i + 1usize;
    }
    self.nfinals = kept;
    // Keep the dead, with what they reach
    i = 0usize;
    while i < ndead {
      imm header = *(dead + i);
      imm h = header as &mut ActorGc;
      if h.flags & flagLarge != 0u32 {
        h.flags = h.flags | flagMarked;
        self.liveNow = self.liveNow + usize.from(h.size);
        self.pushGray(header);
      } else {
        self.copyObject(h);
      }
      i = i + 1usize;
    }
    self.drain();
    // Run their deaths on the copies, newest first, and take them off the list
    i = ndead;
    while i > 0usize {
      i = i - 1usize;
      imm header = ActorRegion.moved(*(dead + i));
      imm rec = (header as &ActorGc).ty as &TypeRecord;
      imm fin = rec.finalize;
      fin((header + ActorRegion.valueOffset(rec)) as *u8);
      self.nfinalized = self.nfinalized + 1usize;
    }
    libc.free(dead as *u8);
  }

  // A collection: the state at 'st', whose type record is 'rec', and what the
  // pending table parks with it, are the roots
  fn collect(self &mut, st *u8, rec *TypeRecord) {
    self.epoch = self.epoch + 1u32;
    if self.epoch == 0u32 {
      self.epoch = 1u32;
    }
    curActorRegion = self as *ActorRegion;
    imm oldChunks = self.first;
    self.first = null;
    self.last = null;
    self.scanChunk = null;
    self.scanAt = 0usize;
    self.nchunks = 0usize;
    self.bytes = 0usize;
    self.liveNow = 0usize;
    // The state: its type's trace hands every reference it holds, among them
    // the records parked by 'await', to 'mark'
    imm r = rec as &TypeRecord;
    if r.flags & 2u32 != 0u32 {
      imm trace = r.trace;
      trace(st, self.epoch);
    }
    self.drain();
    self.finalizeDead();
    // The large objects: freed unless reached; those kept are unmarked again
    mut prev = 0usize;
    mut at = self.large;
    mut nlive = 0usize;
    while at != 0usize {
      imm h = at as &mut ActorGc;
      imm next = h.link;
      if h.flags & flagMarked == 0u32 {
        if prev == 0usize {
          self.large = next;
        } else {
          (prev as &mut ActorGc).link = next;
        }
        libc.free(at as *u8);
        self.nfreed = self.nfreed + 1usize;
      } else {
        h.flags = h.flags & ~flagMarked;
        nlive = nlive + 1usize;
        prev = at;
      }
      at = next;
    }
    self.nlarge = nlive;
    // The old chunks, whole
    mut c = oldChunks;
    while c != null {
      imm next = (c as &Chunk).next;
      libc.free(c as *u8);
      c = next;
    }
    self.ncollections = self.ncollections + 1usize;
    self.live = self.liveNow + self.blocks;
    self.allocated = 0usize;
    // Objects: those copied this collection, the large ones and the revived ones
    // are counted as they were kept
    self.nobjects = self.countObjects();
    curActorRegion = null;
  }

  // The objects the region holds: those in the chunks, found by walking them,
  // and the large ones
  fn countObjects(self &mut) usize {
    mut n = self.nlarge;
    mut c = self.first;
    while c != null {
      mut at = mem.sizeof[Chunk]();
      imm used = (c as &Chunk).used;
      while at < used {
        at = at + usize.from(((c as usize + at) as &ActorGc).size);
        n = n + 1usize;
      }
      c = (c as &Chunk).next;
    }
    n;
  }

  // The actor has died, its state finalized: every object on the final list
  // finalized, newest first, and the region freed
  fn die(self &mut) {
    mut i = self.nfinals;
    while i > 0usize {
      i = i - 1usize;
      imm header = *(self.finals + i);
      imm rec = (header as &ActorGc).ty as &TypeRecord;
      imm fin = rec.finalize;
      fin((header + ActorRegion.valueOffset(rec)) as *u8);
      self.nfinalized = self.nfinalized + 1usize;
    }
    mut c = self.first;
    while c != null {
      imm next = (c as &Chunk).next;
      libc.free(c as *u8);
      c = next;
    }
    mut at = self.large;
    while at != 0usize {
      imm next = (at as &ActorGc).link;
      libc.free(at as *u8);
      at = next;
    }
    libc.free(self.finals as *u8);
    libc.free(self.gray as *u8);
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The region as a MemRegion, and what the message loop calls
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The region functions a collection made in the region calls, each handed the
// MemRegion it holds, which is the 'vt' field of the actor's ActorRegion: one
// pointer past its start
fn blockRegion(r *MemRegion) &mut ActorRegion {
  ((r as usize - mem.sizeof[usize]()) as *ActorRegion) as &mut ActorRegion;
}

fn blockAlloc(r *MemRegion, bytes usize, align usize) *u8 {
  blockRegion(r).blockNew(bytes, align);
}

fn blockResize(r *MemRegion, block *u8, oldBytes usize, newBytes usize, align usize) *u8 {
  blockRegion(r).blockGrow(block, oldBytes, newBytes, align);
}

fn blockRelease(r *MemRegion, block *u8, bytes usize) {
  blockRegion(r).blockGive(block, bytes);
}

// A message has finished: collect, if the bytes allocated since the last
// collection exceed what survived it, and are not under the floor (or if stress
// is on). Only an actor that has asked for its region comes here
fn collectIfDue(g &mut ActorRegion, st *u8, rec *TypeRecord) {
  mut want = stressOn;
  if !want {
    mut threshold = floorBytes;
    if g.live > threshold {
      threshold = g.live;
    }
    want = g.allocated > threshold;
  }
  if want {
    g.collect(st, rec);
  }
}

// A new actor's region, once its Header is where it will stay: pointed at its
// own three functions, which every collection made in it is handed
fn attachRegion(h *Header) {
  (*h).gc.region = (&mut (*h).gc.vt) as *MemRegion;
}

// The actor's region, for the state's generated 'allocRegion'
pub fn regionOf[S, M Sendable](st &mut S) &'static mut ActorRegion {
  imm g = &mut (*headerOf[S, M](st)).gc as *ActorRegion;
  (*g).used = true;
  g as &mut ActorRegion;
}
